UID:0ba47eabf4eabf5b@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261026T223919Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261026
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor obtains consent order 
 against retirement plan fiduciaries of defunct Chicago law firm for failin
 g to distribute $6.4M in assets
DESCRIPTION:CHICAGO – Following a complaint filed by the U.S. Departme
 nt of Labor\, a defunct Chicago law firm’s retirement plan distributed $
 6\,498\,858 in retirement plan assets to 40 employees. The plan’s fiduci
 aries failed to properly terminate the plan and disburse those funds as re
 quired by law.On Aug. 18\, 2026\, the U.S. District Court for the Northern
  District of Illinois\, Eastern Division\, entered a consent order resolvi
 ng the department’s claims against the McCullough\, Campbell & Lane LLP\
 , and the firm’s capital partners David L. Joslyn\, John W. McCullough\,
  F. Dennis Nelson\, and Paul S. Turner. The department alleged that the de
 fendants violated the Employee Retirement Income Security Act by failing 
 to follow a 2019 partnership resolution. The resolution required them to t
 erminate the firm’s retirement plan and distribute the plan’s assets t
 o employees. The defendants have provided proof that plan’s assets were
  distributed to participants\, except for approximately $13\,861 in the em
 ployer-level plan accounts. The consent order requires the plan’s record
 keeper to distribute those funds to its participants and to terminate the 
 plan. “After their law firm closed\, Turner\, McCullough\, Nelson\, and
  Joslyn failed to provide employees with their promised retirement benefit
 s\,” said Employee Benefits Security Administration Regional Director Ke
 lli Hammerl in Chicago. “The Department of Labor helped participants gai
 n access to their retirement savings.” The order permanently bars Turne
 r\, McCullough\, Nelson\, and Joslyn from serving as fiduciaries or servic
 e providers for any ERISA-covered employee benefit plan. The plan transfer
 red account balances belonging to plan participants it could not locate to
  the Pension Benefit Guaranty Corporation’s Missing Participants Progra
 m.Employers can get help complying with the law through EBSA’s correcti
 on programs\, which includes the Voluntary Fiduciary Correction Program. T
 he VFCP allows plan officials to correct violations and avoid excise taxes
  \nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261026
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261026
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ec4f4874878f26c4@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261026T223919Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261026
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor awards $250\,000 in Br
 ookwood-Sago Grants to promote mine safety\, health education
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor today announced th
 e award of $250\,000 in Brookwood-Sago grant funding to two organizations 
 to support education and training initiatives that will help identify and 
 prevent unsafe working conditions in and around the nation’s mines.Admin
 istered by the department’s Mine Safety and Health Administration\, the
  Brookwood-Sago Mine Safety grant program helps recipients create trainin
 g materials\, conduct mine safety training\, and educational programs desi
 gned to protect the safety and health of miners. In awarding the grants\,
  MSHA focused on innovative plans for mine safety and pilot programs that 
 may be useful for miners working in a broad range of positions. The grants
  also put an emphasis on programs and materials targeting workers in small
 er mines that train mine operators and miners about MSHA standards\, and e
 mergency response training.Established under the Mine Improvement and New
  Emergency Response Act of 2006\, the grant program honors the miners los
 t at the Jim Walter Resources #5 mine in Brookwood\, Alabama\, on Sept. 23
 \, 2001\, and at the Sago Mine in Buckhannon\, West Virginia\, on Jan. 2\,
  2006.The recipients of the 2026 Brookwood-Sago grants are as follows:Reci
 pientCityState/TerritoryAmountSaelua Medical Corporation Pago PagoAmerica
 n Samoa$170\,000University of ArizonaTucsonAZ$80\,000\nhttp://www.dol.gov/
 newsroom/releases/msha/msha20261026
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/msha/msha20261026
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
